ained architect  turned photographer who spent six years  visiting 70 institutions in 30 states  they were left abandoned after the  advent of modern medicine and a shift  toward D institutionalization these are  the largest buildings at their time they  dominated the landscape and there was  nothing like them before they were an  American invention his book Asylum  inside the closed world of state mental  hospitals showcases how time Stood Still  when they were emptied out  I saw toothbrushes rooms that had been  left behind as if people just left that  day and never came back  operating theaters movie theaters  bowling alleys sadly most of the places  I photographed are no longer there  when I was working I felt a strong sense  of ownership and proprietorship is  perhaps the final official documentary  the asylums of its time weren't designed  to be frightening  instead the hope was to help heal  patients with their beauty lots of light  open spaces and sprawling grounds a far  cry from the fictionalized versions in  film and TV we see them in movies sort  of all the creepiness but they were  really designed to be something entirely  different right I think the creepiness  comes from the era of architecture and  then when the buildings are shuttered or  closed down the life goes away they  become the perfect backdrop for you know  horror stories and things like that over  the course of 17 years the family  redeveloped more than half of the campus  these like one hundred square foot rooms  have to be made larger for most of our  residential uses but there are still a  handful of buildings that haven't been  touched you actually walk people through  spaces that look like this to try to  convince them that this might be a place  they want to live yeah they had a sign  of waiver that they were entering an  environment with toxic materials and  saying okay now here we are beautiful  building right isn't wouldn't this be a  fantastic place for a homes those people  that didn't run out of the building  screaming were the ones that said they  had a vision and I said I could live  here Susan Krause originally from New  York was one of the first residents  thought it fit my personality I was just  perfect I'd finally found my own did you  have any reservations about  moving into a former asylum no not  really even though I do believe in the  paranormal and I think it's possible  that there are otherworldly creations  out there but no I had none at all and  neither did the team behind the hotel  Henry who reimagined the former Buffalo  State Hospital as a hotel and conference  center in Columbia South Carolina  developers are converting what used to  be a state Asylum into one of the  largest downtown mixed-use real estate  projects in the nation only a fraction  of the original asylums are still  standing today and only a few can see  them the way Susan Krauss can and this  is really special with her own private  spire and a view from the top look at  the view Owen it's decorating just for  us  the Minervini vision that's finally come  to life we had no business doing this  but perseverance and enough people  fighting on our behalf  made it all possible a three-bedroom  condo in that former Asylum recently  sold for nearly $800,000 and if you're  not quite ready to move in they do offer  tours and people are very very curious  so far this year 19,000 people have gone  there though I don't know if I can do it  I think the horror films got to me but  those spaces are and when you go there  you feel the weight you feel the history  and the grounds are stunning on their  own great way to flip some property yeah  and it's hard work lieutenant Thanks

